<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-integral-ww" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Additional Worked-Out Problems</title>
 
     <subsection xml:id="subsec-integral-antiderivatives">
      <title><xref ref="sec-integral-antiderivatives"/> Antiderivatives</title>

     
<example xml:id="ex-antiderivative-linear-combo">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Find the most general antiderivative of
      <me>
        f(x) = 4x^2 - \frac{1}{x^3} + \sin(2x).
      </me>
      Check your answer by differentiating.
    </p>
  </statement>

  <solution>
    <p>
      First rewrite the function using exponents:
    </p>

    <me>
      f(x) = 4x^2 - x^{-3} + \sin(2x).
    </me>

    <p>
      Now use the <xref ref="thm-antiderivative-rules">rules for antiderivatives</xref>
      of powers and trigonometric functions, along with linearity, to build an antiderivative
      term-by-term:
    </p>

    <me>
      F(x) = 4\left(\frac{1}{3}x^3\right) + \frac{1}{2}x^{-2} - \frac{1}{2}\cos(2x) + C
    </me>

    <p>
      We can check our answer by differentiating:
    </p>

    <me>
      \begin{aligned}
      \amp \; \frac{d}{dx}\left(\frac{4x^3}{3} + \frac{1}{2}x^{-2} - \frac{1}{2}\cos(2x) + C\right)\\[6pt]
      \amp=
      \frac{4}{3}\cdot 3x^2 + \frac{1}{2}(-2)x^{-3} - \frac{1}{2}(-\sin(2x))\cdot 2 \\[6pt]
      \amp=
      4x^2 - x^{-3} + \sin(2x) \\[6pt]
      \amp=
      4x^2 - \frac{1}{x^3} + \sin(2x)
      \end{aligned}
    </me>

    <p>
      Since differentiating recovers the original function, the result is confirmed as the most general antiderivative.
    </p>
  </solution>
</example>      

<example xml:id="ex-antiderivative-initial-condition-rational">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Find the antiderivative <m>F(x)</m> of
      <me>
        f(x) = \frac{2}{x^4} - \frac{9}{2x^2}
      </me>
      which satisfies <m>F(-1)=10</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>

  <solution>
    <p>
      First rewrite the function using exponents:
    </p>

    <me>
      f(x) = 2x^{-4} - \frac{9}{2}x^{-2}.
    </me>

    <p>
      Now use the <xref ref="thm-antiderivative-rules">rules for antiderivatives</xref>
      of powers, along with linearity, to build the most general antiderivative term-by-term:
    </p>

    <me>
      F(x) = 2\left(\frac{x^{-3}}{-3}\right) - \frac{9}{2}\left(\frac{x^{-1}}{-1}\right) + C
    </me>

    <p>
      Simplifying gives
    </p>

    <me>
      F(x) = -\frac{2}{3}x^{-3} + \frac{9}{2}x^{-1} + C.
    </me>

    <p>
      Since <m>F(-1)=10</m>, substitute <m>x=-1</m> to find <m>C</m>:
    </p>

    <me>
      \begin{aligned}
      10
      \amp= F(-1) \\[4pt]
      \amp= -\frac{2}{3}(-1)^{-3} + \frac{9}{2}(-1)^{-1} + C \\[4pt]
      \amp= \frac{2}{3} - \frac{9}{2} + C.
      \end{aligned}
    </me>

    <p>
      Solving for <m>C</m>, we get
    </p>

    <me>
      \begin{aligned}
      C
      \amp= 10 - \frac{2}{3} + \frac{9}{2} \\[4pt]
      \amp= \frac{60}{6} - \frac{4}{6} + \frac{27}{6} \\[4pt]
      \amp= \frac{83}{6}.
      \end{aligned}
    </me>

    <p>
      Therefore, the antiderivative is
    </p>

    <me>
      F(x) = -\frac{2}{3}x^{-3} + \frac{9}{2}x^{-1} + \frac{83}{6}.
    </me>

    <p>
      We can check our answer by differentiating and verifying the given value:
    </p>

    <me>
      \begin{aligned}
      F'(x)
      \amp= \frac{d}{dx}\left(-\frac{2}{3}x^{-3} + \frac{9}{2}x^{-1} + \frac{83}{6}\right) \\[4pt]
      \amp= -\frac{2}{3}(-3)x^{-4} + \frac{9}{2}(-1)x^{-2} \\[4pt]
      \amp= 2x^{-4} - \frac{9}{2}x^{-2} \\[4pt]
      \amp= \frac{2}{x^4} - \frac{9}{2x^2}
      \end{aligned}
    </me>

    <me>
      \begin{aligned}
      F(-1)
      \amp= -\frac{2}{3}(-1)^{-3} + \frac{9}{2}(-1)^{-1} + \frac{83}{6} \\[4pt]
      \amp= \frac{2}{3} - \frac{9}{2} + \frac{83}{6} \\[4pt]
      \amp= 10.
      \end{aligned}
    </me>

    <p>
      Since <m>F'(x)=f(x)</m> and <m>F(-1)=10</m>, the result is confirmed.
    </p>
  </solution>
</example>

<example xml:id="ex-antiderivative-radical-quotient">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Find the most general antiderivative of
      <me>
        f(x) = \frac{3\sqrt{x}-2x^3}{x}.
      </me>
      Check your answer by differentiating.
    </p>
  </statement>

  <solution>
    <p>
      First simplify the expression and rewrite using exponents:
    </p>

    <me>
      f(x) = \frac{3\sqrt{x}}{x} - \frac{2x^3}{x} = 3x^{-1/2} - 2x^2.
    </me>

    <p>
      Now use the <xref ref="thm-antiderivative-rules">rules for antiderivatives</xref>
      of powers, along with linearity, to build an antiderivative term-by-term:
    </p>

    <me>
      F(x) = 3\left(\frac{x^{1/2}}{1/2}\right) - 2\left(\frac{x^3}{3}\right) + C
    </me>

    <p>
      Simplifying gives
    </p>

    <me>
      F(x) = 6x^{1/2} - \frac{2}{3}x^3 + C.
    </me>

    <p>
      We can check our answer by differentiating:
    </p>

    <me>
      \begin{aligned}
      \amp \; \frac{d}{dx}\left(6x^{1/2} - \frac{2}{3}x^3 + C\right) \\[6pt]
      \amp= 6\left(\frac{1}{2}x^{-1/2}\right) - \frac{2}{3}(3x^2) \\[6pt]
      \amp= 3x^{-1/2} - 2x^2.
      \end{aligned}
    </me>

    <p>
      Since differentiating recovers the original function, the result is confirmed as the most general antiderivative.
    </p>
  </solution>
</example>


<example xml:id="ex-antiderivative-motion-position">
  <statement>
    <p>
      An object moves along a coordinate line with velocity
      <me>
        v(t) = 6t^2 - 7
      </me>
      (in units per second). Its initial position is 5 units to the left of the origin.
      Find the position of the object after 3 seconds.
    </p>
  </statement>

  <solution>
    <p>
      Let <m>s(t)</m> be the position of the object at time <m>t</m>.
      Since velocity is the derivative of position, we have
    </p>

    <me>
      s'(t) = v(t) = 6t^2 - 7.
    </me>

    <p>
      Thus <m>s(t)</m> is an antiderivative of <m>6t^2 - 7</m>.
      Using the <xref ref="thm-antiderivative-rules">rules for antiderivatives</xref>,
      we obtain the most general form:
    </p>

    <me>
      s(t) = 2t^3 - 7t + C.
    </me>

    <p>
      We are given that the initial position is 5 units to the left of the origin, so
      <m>s(0) = -5</m>. Substituting:
    </p>

    <me>
      \begin{aligned}
      s(0)
      -5 \amp= 2(0)^3 - 7(0) + C \\[4pt]
      -5 \amp= C.
      \end{aligned}
    </me>

    <p>
      Therefore <m>C = -5</m>, and the position function is
    </p>

    <me>
      s(t) = 2t^3 - 7t - 5.
    </me>

    <p>
      To find the position after 3 seconds, evaluate:
    </p>

    <me>
      \begin{aligned}
      s(3)
      \amp= 2(3)^3 - 7(3) - 5 \\[4pt]
      \amp= 54 - 21 - 5 \\[4pt]
      \amp= 28.
      \end{aligned}
    </me>

    <p>
      Since the position is positive, the object is 28 units to the right of the origin after 3 seconds.
    </p>

    <p>
      We can check our work by differentiating:
    </p>

    <me>
      \begin{aligned}
      s'(t)
      \amp= \frac{d}{dt}\left(2t^3 - 7t - 5\right) \\[4pt]
      \amp= 6t^2 - 7 \\[4pt]
      \amp= v(t).
      \end{aligned}
    </me>

    <p>
      Since differentiating recovers the given velocity, the result is confirmed.
    </p>
  </solution>
</example>


<example xml:id="ex-antiderivative-initial-value-problem">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Solve the initial value problem: 
      <me>
        \frac{dy}{dt} = \frac{2}{t^2+1} - \frac{9}{2t}, \quad y(-1)=0.
      </me>
    </p>
  </statement>

  <solution>
    <p>  Our goal is to find a function <m>y(t)</m> such that <m>y' = \frac{2}{t^2+1} - \frac{9}{2t}</m> and <m>y(-1)=0</m>. </p>
    <p> First, we find the most general antiderivative of the right-hand side by using the 
      <xref ref="thm-antiderivative-rules">rules for antiderivatives</xref>.
    </p>
    
    <me>
      y(t) = 2\arctan(t) - \frac{9}{2}\ln|t| + C.
    </me>

    <p>
      Now we use the initial condition <m>y(-1)=0</m> to solve for <m>C</m>:
    </p>
    <me>
      \begin{aligned}
      0
      \amp= y(-1) \\[4pt]
      0\amp= 2\arctan(-1) - \frac{9}{2}\ln|-1| + C \\[4pt]
      0\amp= 2\left(-\frac{\pi}{4}\right) - 0 + C \\[4pt]
      0\amp= -\frac{\pi}{2} + C \\[4pt]
      \frac{\pi}{2} \amp = C
      \end{aligned}
    </me>

    <p>
      Therefore, the solution to the initial value problem is
    </p>
    <me>
      y(t) = 2\arctan(t) - \frac{9}{2}\ln|t| + \frac{\pi}{2}.
    </me>

  </solution>
</example>


<example xml:id="ex-antiderivative-population-model">
  <statement>
    <p>
      A small population is decreasing over time. Let <m>P(t)</m> be the population after <m>t</m> years.
      Suppose the rate of change is modeled by
      <me>
        P'(t) = -15e^{-0.2t}.
      </me>
    </p>

      <ol marker="(a)">
        <li> 
    <p>
      Write a general formula for <m>P(t)</m> assuming an unknown initial population.
    </p>
        </li>
      <li>
    <p>
      If the initial population is <m>500</m>, find the specific formula for <m>P(t)</m>.
    </p>
        </li>
      <li>
    <p>
      According to the model, what will the population be after 3 years?
    </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
  </statement>

  <solution>
    <p>
      (a) Since <m>P'(t)</m> is given, the function <m>P(t)</m> is an antiderivative of
      <m>-15e^{-0.2t}</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Using the <xref ref="thm-antiderivative-rules">rules for antiderivatives</xref>, we obtain:
    </p>

    <me>
      P(t) = -15\left(\frac{1}{-0.2}e^{-0.2t}\right) + C.
    </me>

    <p>
      Simplifying gives
    </p>

    <me>
      P(t) = 75e^{-0.2t} + C.
    </me>

    <p>
      This is the general formula.
    </p>

    <p>
      (b) Now use the initial condition <m>P(0)=500</m>:
    </p>

    <me>
      \begin{aligned}
      500
      \amp= P(0) \\[4pt]
      500 \amp= 75e^{0} + C \\[4pt]
      500 \amp= 75 + C\\[4pt]
      425 \amp= C
      \end{aligned}
    </me>

    <p>
      Therefore, the specific formula is
    </p>

    <me>
      P(t) = 75e^{-0.2t} + 425.
    </me>

    <p>
      (c) Now evaluate at <m>t=3</m>:
    </p>

    <me>
      P(3) = 75e^{-0.6} + 425 
    </me>

    <p>
      With access to a calculator (if one is permitted), we can find a decimal approximation to be about <m>466</m>.
    </p>
   
  </solution>
</example>








    </subsection>

       <subsection xml:id="subsec-integral-riemannSums">
      <title><xref ref="sec-integral-riemannSums"/> Riemann Sums and Areas</title>

      

<example xml:id="ex-riemann-all-sums">
  <title>Comparing Left, Right, Upper, and Lower Sums</title>

  <statement>
    <p>
      Estimate the area under the graph of 
      <m>f(x)=5-x^2</m> on the interval <m>[-2,1]</m> using 
      <m>n=3</m> rectangles of equal width.
    </p>


  <image width="80%">
  <shortdescription>
    Graph of a downward-opening parabola labeled f(x) with shaded area under the curve from x = -2 to x = 1
  </shortdescription>
    <latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=2]
	\draw[fill=green!20] plot[domain=-2:1, smooth] (\x, {5-(\x)^2}) -- (1,0)--(-2,0) -- (-2,1);
	\draw[->, thick] (-2.5,0) -- (2.5,0) node [right] {$x$} ;
	\draw[->, thick] (0,0) -- (0,5.5);
	\draw[very thick] plot[domain=-2.3:2.3, smooth] (\x, {5-(\x)^2});
	\draw (2.2,1.5) node {$f(x)$};
	\foreach \x in {-2,...,2}
	\draw (\x,2pt)--(\x,-2pt)node[below, font=\small] {$\x$};
	
\end{tikzpicture}
 ]]>
    </latex-image>
  </image>

    <p>
      Compute:
    </p>
    <ol marker="(a)">
      <li>the left Riemann sum</li>
      <li>the right Riemann sum</li>
      <li>an overestimate (upper sum)</li>
      <li>an underestimate (lower sum)</li>
    </ol>
  </statement>

  <solution>
    <p>
      First compute the width of each rectangle:
    </p>

    <me>
      \displaystyle \Delta x=\frac{1-(-2)}{3}=1.
    </me>

    <p>
      The partition points are:
    </p>

    <me>
      -2,\;-1,\;0,\;1.
    </me>

    <p>
      The subintervals are:
    </p>

    <me>
      [-2,-1],\quad [-1,0],\quad [0,1].
    </me>

    <p><alert>(a) Left Riemann Sum</alert></p>

    <me>
      \begin{aligned}
      f(-2)\amp=1,\\
      f(-1)\amp=4,\\
      f(0)\amp=5
      \end{aligned}
    </me>

    <me>
      \displaystyle
      \text{Left Riemann Sum }  =(1+4+5)(1)=10.
    </me>

    <p> Graphically, the left sum corresponds to rectangles that touch the curve at the left endpoints of the subintervals. 
    </p> 

      <image width="80%">
  <shortdescription>
    Graph of a downward-opening parabola labeled f(x) with shaded area under the curve from x = -2 to x = 1, 
    with rectangles drawn touching the curve at the left endpoints of the subintervals [-2,-1], [-1,0], and [0,1]
  </shortdescription>
    <latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=2] %left
	\draw[fill=green!20] plot[domain=-2:1, smooth] (\x, {5-(\x)^2}) -- (1,0)--(-2,0) -- (-2,1);
	\draw[->, thick] (-2.5,0) -- (2.5,0) node [right] {$x$} ;
	\draw[->, thick] (0,0) -- (0,5.5);
	\draw[very thick] plot[domain=-2.3:2.3, smooth] (\x, {5-(\x)^2});
	\draw (2.2,1.5) node {$f(x)$};
	\foreach \x in {-2,...,2}
	\draw (\x,2pt)--(\x,-2pt)node[below, font=\small] {$\x$};
	
	\draw[black, fill=blue, fill opacity=.2] (-2,0) rectangle (-1,1); 
	\draw[black, fill=blue, fill opacity=.2] (-1,0) rectangle (0,4); 
	\draw[black, fill=blue, fill opacity=.2] (0,0) rectangle (1,5); 
	\draw[dashed] (-1.5,0) -- (-1.5,1) node [right, pos=.5] {$1$};
	\draw[dashed] (-.5,0) -- (-.5,4) node [right, pos=.5] {$4$};
	\draw[dashed] (.5,0) -- (.5,5) node [right, pos=.5] {$5$};
	\draw (-2,1) node {$\bullet$};
	\draw (-1,4) node {$\bullet$};
	\draw (0,5) node {$\bullet$};
\end{tikzpicture}
 ]]>
    </latex-image>
  </image>
    
    <p><alert>(b) Right Riemann Sum</alert></p>

    <me>
      \begin{aligned}
      f(-1)\amp=4,\\
      f(0)\amp=5,\\
      f(1)\amp=4
      \end{aligned}
    </me>

    <me>
      \displaystyle
      \text{Right Riemann Sum }=(4+5+4)(1)=13.
    </me>

    <p>  Graphically, the right sum corresponds to rectangles that touch the curve at the right endpoints of the subintervals.
    </p>

     <image width="80%">
  <shortdescription>
    Graph of a downward-opening parabola labeled f(x) with shaded area under the curve from x = -2 to x = 1, 
    with rectangles drawn touching the curve at the right endpoints of the subintervals [-2,-1], [-1,0], and [0,1]
  </shortdescription>
    <latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=2] %right
	\draw[fill=green!20] plot[domain=-2:1, smooth] (\x, {5-(\x)^2}) -- (1,0)--(-2,0) -- (-2,1);
	\draw[->, thick] (-2.5,0) -- (2.5,0) node [right] {$x$} ;
	\draw[->, thick] (0,0) -- (0,5.5);
	\draw[very thick] plot[domain=-2.3:2.3, smooth] (\x, {5-(\x)^2});
	\draw (2.2,1.5) node {$f(x)$};
	\foreach \x in {-2,...,2}
	\draw (\x,2pt)--(\x,-2pt)node[below, font=\small] {$\x$};
	\draw[black, fill=blue, fill opacity=.2] (-2,0) rectangle (-1,4); 
	\draw[black, fill=blue, fill opacity=.2] (-1,0) rectangle (0,5); 
	\draw[black, fill=blue, fill opacity=.2] (0,0) rectangle (1,4); 
	\draw[dashed] (-1.5,0) -- (-1.5,4) node [right, pos=.5] {$4$};
	\draw[dashed] (-.5,0) -- (-.5,5) node [right, pos=.5] {$5$};
	\draw[dashed] (.5,0) -- (.5,4) node [right, pos=.5] {$4$};
	\draw (-2,1) node {$\bullet$};
	\draw (-1,4) node {$\bullet$};
	\draw (1,4) node {$\bullet$};
\end{tikzpicture}
 ]]>
    </latex-image>
  </image>

    <p><alert>(c) Upper Sum (Overestimate)</alert></p>

    <p>
      To determine upper and lower sums, note that <m>f(x)=5-x^2</m> is increasing on <m>[-2,0]</m>
      and decreasing on <m>[0,1]</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Use the maximum value on each subinterval:
    </p>

    <me>
      \begin{aligned}
      f(-1)\amp=4,\\
      f(0)\amp=5,\\
      f(0)\amp=5
      \end{aligned}
    </me>

    <me>
      \displaystyle
      \text{Upper Sum }=(4+5+5)(1)=14.
    </me>

         <image width="80%">
  <shortdescription>
    Graph of a downward-opening parabola labeled f(x) with shaded area under the curve from x = -2 to x = 1, 
    with rectangles drawn touching the curve at the maximum value on each subinterval: at x = -1 for [-2,-1], at x = 0 for [-1,0],
    and at x = 0 for [0,1]
  </shortdescription>
    <latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=2] %upper
	\draw[fill=green!20] plot[domain=-2:1, smooth] (\x, {5-(\x)^2}) -- (1,0)--(-2,0) -- (-2,1);
	\draw[->, thick] (-2.5,0) -- (2.5,0) node [right] {$x$} ;
	\draw[->, thick] (0,0) -- (0,5.5);
	\draw[very thick] plot[domain=-2.3:2.3, smooth] (\x, {5-(\x)^2});
	\draw (2.2,1.5) node {$f(x)$};
	\foreach \x in {-2,...,2}
	\draw (\x,2pt)--(\x,-2pt)node[below, font=\small] {$\x$};
	\draw[black, fill=blue, fill opacity=.2] (-2,0) rectangle (-1,4); 
	\draw[black, fill=blue, fill opacity=.2] (-1,0) rectangle (0,5); 
	\draw[black, fill=blue, fill opacity=.2] (0,0) rectangle (1,5); 
		\draw[dashed] (-1.5,0) -- (-1.5,4) node [right, pos=.5] {$4$};
	\draw[dashed] (-.5,0) -- (-.5,5) node [right, pos=.5] {$5$};
	\draw[dashed] (.5,0) -- (.5,5) node [right, pos=.5] {$5$};
	\draw (-1,4) node {$\bullet$};
	\draw (0,5) node {$\bullet$};
\end{tikzpicture}
 ]]>
    </latex-image>
  </image>
    

    <p><alert>(d) Lower Sum (Underestimate)</alert></p>

    <p>
      Use the minimum value on each subinterval:
    </p>

    <me>
      \begin{aligned}
      f(-2)\amp=1,\\
      f(-1)\amp=4,\\
      f(1)\amp =4
      \end{aligned}
    </me>

    <me>
      \displaystyle
      \text{Lower Sum }=(1+4+4)(1)=9.
    </me>


       <image width="80%">
  <shortdescription>
    Graph of a downward-opening parabola labeled f(x) with shaded area under the curve from x = -2 to x = 1, 
    with rectangles drawn touching the curve at the minimum value on each subinterval: 
    at x = -2 for [-2,-1], at x = -1 for [-1,0], and at x = 1 for [0,1]
  </shortdescription>
    <latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=2] %lower
	\draw[fill=green!20] plot[domain=-2:1, smooth] (\x, {5-(\x)^2}) -- (1,0)--(-2,0) -- (-2,1);
	\draw[->, thick] (-2.5,0) -- (2.5,0) node [right] {$x$} ;
	\draw[->, thick] (0,0) -- (0,5.5);
	\draw[very thick] plot[domain=-2.3:2.3, smooth] (\x, {5-(\x)^2});
	\draw (2.2,1.5) node {$f(x)$};
	\foreach \x in {-2,...,2}
	\draw (\x,2pt)--(\x,-2pt)node[below, font=\small] {$\x$};
	\draw[black, fill=blue, fill opacity=.2] (-2,0) rectangle (-1,1); 
	\draw[black, fill=blue, fill opacity=.2] (-1,0) rectangle (0,4); 
	\draw[black, fill=blue, fill opacity=.2] (0,0) rectangle (1,4); 
		\draw[dashed] (-1.5,0) -- (-1.5,1) node [right, pos=.5] {$1$};
	\draw[dashed] (-.5,0) -- (-.5,4) node [right, pos=.5] {$4$};
	\draw[dashed] (.5,0) -- (.5,4) node [right, pos=.5] {$4$};
	\draw (-2,1) node {$\bullet$};
	\draw (-1,4) node {$\bullet$};
	\draw (1,4) node {$\bullet$};
\end{tikzpicture}
 ]]>
    </latex-image>
  </image>


    <p>
      So we have:
    </p>

    <me>
      \displaystyle
      \text{Lower Sum}=9 \le \text{Actual Area} \le 14=\text{Upper Sum}.
    </me>

    <p>
      The left and right sums, <m>10</m> and <m>13</m>, fall between these bounds.
    </p>

  </solution>
</example>

<example xml:id="ex-riemann-velocity-table">
  <title>Estimating Distance from a Velocity Table</title>

  <statement>
    <p>
      The following table gives the velocity <m>v(t)</m> (in ft/s) of a particle 
      moving along a horizontal axis at different times <m>t</m> (in seconds).
    </p>

    <me>
      \begin{array}{c|ccccc}
      t &amp; 20 &amp; 23 &amp; 26 &amp; 29 &amp; 32 \\
      \hline
      v(t) &amp; 15 &amp; 10 &amp; 7 &amp; 9 &amp; 12
      \end{array}
    </me>

    <p>
      Estimate the distance traveled by the particle from <m>t=20</m> to <m>t=32</m>
      using 4 subintervals of equal width.
    </p>

    <ol marker="(a)">
      <li>Use left endpoints.</li>
      <li>Use right endpoints.</li>
      <li>Find an overestimate of the distance assuming all minimums and maximums are at the endpoints.</li>
    </ol>
  </statement>

  <solution>
    <p>
      First compute the width of each subinterval:
    </p>

    <me>
      \displaystyle \Delta t = \frac{32-20}{4} = 3.
    </me>

    <p><alert>(a) Left Endpoint Approximation</alert></p>

    <p>
      The left endpoints are:
    </p>

    <me>
      20,\;23,\;26,\;29.
    </me>

    <p>
      So the estimated distance is:
    </p>

    <me>
      \begin{aligned}
      \text{Distance} 
      &amp;\approx 3\bigl[v(20)+v(23)+v(26)+v(29)\bigr] \\
      &amp;= 3(15+10+7+9) \\
      &amp;= 3(41) = 123 \text{ ft}. 
      \end{aligned}
    </me>

    <p><alert>(b) Right Endpoint Approximation</alert></p>

    <p>
      The right endpoints are:
    </p>

    <me>
      23,\;26,\;29,\;32.
    </me>

    <p>
      So the estimated distance is:
    </p>

    <me>
      \begin{aligned}
      \text{Distance} 
      &amp;\approx 3\bigl[v(23)+v(26)+v(29)+v(32)\bigr] \\
      &amp;= 3(10+7+9+12) \\
      &amp;= 3(38) = 114 \text{ ft}.
      \end{aligned}
    </me>

    <p><alert>(c) Overestimate</alert></p>

    <p>
      To find an overestimate, take the maximum velocity on each subinterval. 
      Because we are told that all minimums and maximums are at the endpoints, we only need to look at the values in the table:
    </p>

    <me>
      \begin{array}{c|cccc}
      \text{Interval} &amp; [20,23] &amp; [23,26] &amp; [26,29] &amp; [29,32] \\
      \hline
      \text{Max } v(t) &amp; 15 &amp; 10 &amp; 9 &amp; 12
      \end{array}
    </me>

    <p>
      So the overestimate is:
    </p>

    <me>
      \begin{aligned}
      \text{Distance} 
      &amp;\approx 3(15+10+9+12) \\
      &amp;= 3(46) = 138 \text{ ft}.
      \end{aligned}
    </me>

    <p>
      Therefore, the distance traveled is approximately between 
      <m>114</m> feet and <m>138</m> feet, with the left sum giving <m>123</m> feet.
    </p>

  </solution>
</example>




    </subsection>









       <subsection xml:id="subsec-integral-defInt">
      <title><xref ref="sec-integral-defInt"/> Definite Integrals</title>

<example xml:id="ex-accum-temp-rate">
<statement>
  <p>
    The graph of the rate of change of temperature <m>r(t)</m> (in degrees Fahrenheit per hour)
    over a 5-hour period can be viewed at the following link:
    <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/hymf4b8vsh">Interactive Desmos Graph</url>.</p>
    <p> 
    Here, <m>t</m> is measured in hours. Positive values of <m>r(t)</m> mean the temperature is increasing,
    while negative values mean the temperature is decreasing.
  </p>

  <p>
    Use the interactive graph to explore the accumulation of <m>r(t)</m> and answer the following questions.
  </p>

  <ol marker="(a)">
    <li>
      If the temperature is <m>60^\circ</m> at <m>t=0</m>, what is the temperature after 5 hours?
    </li>
    <li>
      After how many hours is the temperature highest?
    </li>
    <li>
      What is the highest temperature reached during the 5-hour period?
    </li>
  </ol>

</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    (a) The temperature after 5 hours is the initial temperature plus the accumulation of the rate of change over the 5-hour period. 
    This can be expressed as:
  </p>
  <me>
    \text{Temperature}\Big|_{t=5} = 60 + \int_0^5 r(t)\,dt
  </me>
  <p>
    Using the interactive graph, we can estimate the value of the integral. 
    By adjusting the bounds of integration and observing the accumulated area under the curve of <m>r(t)</m>, we find that:
  </p>
  <me>
    \int_0^5 r(t)\,dt \approx 5.54.
  </me>
  <p>
    Therefore, the temperature after 5 hours is approximately:
  </p>
  <me>
    \text{Temperature}\Big|_{t=5} \approx 60 + 5.54 = 65.54^\circ.
  </me>
  <p> (b) To find when the temperature is highest, we need to look for the time at which the accumulation of <m>r(t)</m> reaches its maximum. 
    This occurs when the integral of <m>r(t)</m> from 0 to <m>t</m> is at its maximum value. 
    By exploring the graph, we can see that the temperature reaches its highest point around <m>t=4</m> hours.
  </p>
  <p>
    (c) To find the highest temperature reached, we can evaluate the temperature at <m>t=4</m> hours:
  </p>
  <me>
    \text{Temperature}\Big|_{t=4} = 60 + \int_0^4 r(t)\,dt
  </me>
  <p>
    Using the interactive graph, we estimate:
  </p>
  <me>
    \int_0^4 r(t)\,dt \approx 7.28.
  </me>
  <p>
    Therefore, the highest temperature reached during the 5-hour period is approximately:
  </p>
  <me>
    \text{Temperature}\Big|_{t=4} \approx 60 + 7.28 = 67.28^\circ.
  </me>
</solution>

</example>      


<example xml:id="ex-area-abs-signed">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Evaluate the integral below by interpreting it in terms of areas.
      In other words, sketch the graph of the integrand and compute the
      integral using geometry.
    </p>

    <me>
      \displaystyle \int_{-1}^{3} |3x-6|\,dx
    </me>
  </statement>

  <solution>
    <p> First let's sketch the function <m>f(x)=|3x-6|</m>. These absolute value functions are V-shaped, 
      and the vertex occurs where the expression inside the absolute value is zero.
    </p>
    <p>
      Setting <m>3x-6=0</m> gives <m>x=2</m>. So the vertex is at the point <m>(2,0)</m>.
    </p>
    <p> We can also plot the endpoints of the interval:
      <m>f(-1)=|3(-1)-6|=9</m> and <m>f(3)=|3(3)-6|=3</m>. and connect these points with straight lines to get the graph of <m>f(x)</m>.
    </p>

  <image width="60%">
  <shortdescription>
Graph of the function f(x) = |3x - 6|, which is a V-shaped graph with a vertex at (2, 0), and points at (-1, 9) and (3, 3).
  </shortdescription>
    <latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=2]
	\draw[fill=green!20] (-1,9) -- (2,0) -- (3,3) -- (3,0) -- (-1,0) -- cycle;
	\draw (-1,9) node{$\bullet$} -- (2,0) -- (3,3) node{$\bullet$};
	\draw[->, thick] (-1.5,0) -- (3.5,0) node [right] {$x$} ;
	\draw[->, thick] (0,0) -- (0,9.5);
	%\draw[very thick] plot[domain=-1.3:3.3, smooth] (\x, {5-(\x)^2});
	\draw (2.2,1.5) node {$f(x)$};
	\foreach \x in {-1,...,3}
	\draw (\x,2pt)--(\x,-2pt)node[below, font=\small] {$\x$};
	\foreach \x in {1,...,9}
	\draw (2pt, \x)--(-2pt,\x)node[left, font=\small] {$\x$};
\end{tikzpicture}
 ]]>
    </latex-image>
  </image>

  <p>We can see from the graph that the region under the function forms 2 triangles both above the <m>x</m>-axis. 
    The area of the triangle on the left is:</p>
  <me>
  \text{Area}_1 = \frac{1}{2} \cdot \text{base} \cdot \text{height} = \frac{1}{2} \cdot 3 \cdot 9 = \frac{27}{2}.
  </me>
  <p>
    The area of the triangle on the right is:
  </p>
  <me>
  \text{Area}_2 = \frac{1}{2} \cdot \text{base} \cdot \text{height} = \frac{1}{2} \cdot 1 \cdot 3 = \frac{3}{2}.  
  </me>
  <p>
    Therefore, the value of the integral is the sum of these two areas:
  </p>
  <me>
  \int_{-1}^{3} |3x-6|\,dx = \text{Area}_1 + \text{Area}_2 = \frac{27}{2} + \frac{3}{2} = 15.
  </me> 
      
  
  </solution>
</example>

    </subsection>

       <subsection xml:id="subsec-integral-FTC">
      <title><xref ref="sec-integral-FTC"/> The Fundamental Theorem of Calculus</title>

<example xml:id="ex-definite-integral-polynomial">

  <statement>
    <p>
      Evaluate the definite integral:
    </p>

    <me>
      \displaystyle \int_{-1}^{3} 2(x+1)^2\,dx
    </me>
  </statement>

  <solution>
    <p>
      First expand the integrand:
    </p>

    <me>
      2(x+1)^2 = 2(x^2+2x+1) = 2x^2+4x+2.
    </me>

    <p>
      Now apply the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus:
    </p>

    <me>
      \begin{aligned}
      \displaystyle \int_{-1}^{3} 2(x+1)^2\,dx
      &amp;= \displaystyle \int_{-1}^{3} (2x^2+4x+2)\,dx \\
      &amp;= \left[\frac{2x^3}{3} + \frac{4x^2}{2} + 2x \right]_{-1}^{3}.
      \end{aligned}
    </me>

    <p>
      Evaluate at the bounds:
    </p>

    <me>
      \begin{aligned}
      &amp;= \left( \frac{2(3)^3}{3} + 2(3)^2 + 2(3) \right)
      - \left( \frac{2(-1)^3}{3} + 2(-1)^2 + 2(-1) \right) \\
      &amp;= (18 + 18 + 6) - \left( -\frac{2}{3} + 2 - 2 \right) \\
      &amp;= 42 - \left( -\frac{2}{3} \right) \\
      &amp;= \frac{128}{3}.
      \end{aligned}
    </me>

    <p>
      Therefore,
    </p>

    <me>
      \displaystyle \int_{-1}^{3} 2(x+1)^2\,dx = \frac{128}{3}.
    </me>

  </solution>
</example>


<example xml:id="ex-area-parabola">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Use a definite integral to find the area of the region that lies under the curve
      <m>y = 12 - 4x^2</m> and above the <m>x</m>-axis.
    </p>
  </statement>

  <solution>
    <p>
      First determine where the curve intersects the <m>x</m>-axis by solving:
    </p>

    <me>
      12 - 4x^2 = 0.
    </me>

    <me>
      x^2 = 3 \quad \Rightarrow \quad x = \pm \sqrt{3}.
    </me>

    <p>
      The curve is above the <m>x</m>-axis on the interval <m>(-\sqrt{3}, \sqrt{3})</m>,
      so the area is given by:
    </p>

    <me>
      \displaystyle \int_{-\sqrt{3}}^{\sqrt{3}} (12 - 4x^2)\,dx.
    </me>

    <p>
      Evaluate the integral and simplify:
    </p>

    <me>
      \begin{aligned}
      &amp; 
      \displaystyle \int_{-\sqrt{3}}^{\sqrt{3}} (12 - 4x^2)\,dx \\
      &amp;= \left[ 12x - \frac{4x^3}{3} \right]_{-\sqrt{3}}^{\sqrt{3}} \\
      &amp;= \left( 12\sqrt{3} - \frac{4(\sqrt{3})^3}{3} \right)
      - \left( -12\sqrt{3} - \frac{4(-\sqrt{3})^3}{3} \right).\\
      &amp;= \left( 12\sqrt{3} - \frac{4 \cdot 3\sqrt{3}}{3} \right)
      - \left( -12\sqrt{3} + \frac{4 \cdot 3\sqrt{3}}{3} \right) \\
      &amp;= (12\sqrt{3} - 4\sqrt{3}) - (-12\sqrt{3} + 4\sqrt{3}) \\
      &amp;= 8\sqrt{3} - (-8\sqrt{3}) \\
      &amp;= 16\sqrt{3}.
      \end{aligned}
    </me>

    <p>
      Therefore, the area of the region is
      <m>16\sqrt{3}</m>.
    </p>

  </solution>
</example>



<example xml:id="ex-abs-value-integral">

  <statement>
    <p>
      Evaluate the definite integral:
    </p>

    <me>
      \displaystyle \int_{-2}^{1} |x+1|\,dx
    </me>
  </statement>

  <solution>

    <p> We saw in <xref ref="ex-area-abs-signed">a previous example</xref> that this can also be solved by 
      using a geometric approach, but here we will evaluate the integral using the definition of the absolute value 
      function and the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus. Of course if such a problem appears on an exam and it 
      does not specify a method, you can choose either approach.
    </p>
    <p>
      First determine where the expression inside the absolute value is zero:
    </p>

    <me>
      x+1=0 \quad \Rightarrow \quad x=-1.
    </me>

    <p>
      So we write <m>|x+1|</m> as a piecewise function:
    </p>

    <me>
      |x+1|=
      \begin{cases}
      -(x+1), &amp; x &lt; -1, \\
      x+1, &amp; x \ge -1.
      \end{cases}
    </me>

    <p>
      Split the integral at <m>x=-1</m>:
    </p>

    <me>
      \displaystyle \int_{-2}^{1} |x+1|\,dx
      = \displaystyle \int_{-2}^{-1} -(x+1)\,dx
      + \displaystyle \int_{-1}^{1} (x+1)\,dx.
    </me>

    <p>
      Evaluate each integral:
    </p>

    <me>
      \begin{aligned}
      \displaystyle \int_{-2}^{-1} -(x+1)\,dx
      &amp;= \left[-\left(\frac{x^2}{2}+x\right)\right]_{-2}^{-1}, \\
      \displaystyle \int_{-1}^{1} (x+1)\,dx
      &amp;= \left[\frac{x^2}{2}+x\right]_{-1}^{1}.
      \end{aligned}
    </me>

    <p>
      Compute the values:
    </p>

    <me>
      \begin{aligned}
      \displaystyle \int_{-2}^{-1} -(x+1)\,dx
      &amp;= -\left[\left(\frac{(-1)^2}{2}+(-1)\right)
      - \left(\frac{(-2)^2}{2}+(-2)\right)\right] \\
      &amp;= -\left[\left(\frac{1}{2}-1\right) - (2-2)\right]
      = -\left(-\frac{1}{2}\right)
      = \frac{1}{2},\\
      \displaystyle \int_{-1}^{1} (x+1)\,dx
      &amp;= \left[\left(\frac{1}{2}+1\right)
      - \left(\frac{1}{2}-1\right)\right] \\
      &amp;= \frac{3}{2} - \left(-\frac{1}{2}\right)
      = 2.
      \end{aligned}
    </me>

    <p>
      Add the results:
    </p>

    <me>
      \displaystyle \int_{-2}^{1} |x+1|\,dx
      = \frac{1}{2} + 2 = \frac{5}{2}.
    </me>
  </solution>
</example>


<example xml:id="ex-definite-integral-trig">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Evaluate the integral <m> \displaystyle \int_{0}^{\pi/12} \sec(3x)\tan(3x) \,dx</m> 
    </p>
  </statement>
  <solution> 
    <p>
      Thinking back to <xref ref="thm-antiderivative-rules">our antiderivative rules</xref>, we know that the derivative of <m>\sec(3x)</m> is <m>3\sec(3x)\tan(3x)</m>.
      So we can use the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus to evaluate the integral:
    </p>
    <me>
      \begin{aligned}
      \displaystyle \int_{0}^{\pi/12} \sec(3x)\tan(3x) \,dx
      &amp;= \left[\frac{\sec(3x)}{3}\right]_{0}^{\pi/12} \\
      &amp;= \frac{\sec(\pi/4)}{3} - \frac{\sec(0)}{3} \\
      &amp;= \frac{\sqrt{2}}{3} - \frac{1}{3} \\
      &amp;= \frac{\sqrt{2}-1}{3}.
      \end{aligned}
    </me>
  </solution>
</example>



    </subsection>

       <subsection xml:id="subsec-integral-net">
      <title><xref ref="sec-integral-net"/> Net Change Theorem and Indefinite Integrals</title>

<example xml:id="ex-units-integral-mixed">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Complete each statement below by determining the correct units of the definite integral.
    </p>

    <ol marker="(a)">
      <li>
        If <m>h(t)</m> is measured in degrees Fahrenheit per hour and <m>t</m> is measured in hours, then
        <m>\displaystyle \int_a^b h(t)\,dt</m> has what units?
      </li>

      <li>
        If <m>k(x)</m> is measured in pounds per square foot and <m>x</m> is measured in feet, then
        <m>\displaystyle \int_a^b k(x)\,dx</m> has what units?
      </li>
    </ol>
  </statement>

  <solution>
    <p>
      (a) The units of <m>h(t)\,dt</m> are the product of the units of <m>h(t)</m> and <m>dt</m>. 
      Since <m>h(t)</m> is in degrees Fahrenheit per hour and <m>dt</m> is in hours, the units of the integral are:
    </p>

    <me>
      \text{degrees Fahrenheit per hour} \times \text{hours} = \text{degrees Fahrenheit}.
    </me>

    <p>
      (b) The units of <m>k(x)\,dx</m> are the product of the units of <m>k(x)</m> and <m>dx</m>. 
      Since <m>k(x)</m> is in pounds per square foot and <m>dx</m> is in feet, the units of the integral are:
    </p>

    <me>
      \text{pounds per square foot} \times \text{feet} = \text{pounds per foot}.
    </me>
    </solution>
  </example>
      


<example xml:id="ex-mice-race-charlie-frank">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Two mice, Charlie and Frank, race down a straight hallway as far as they can for 30 seconds.
      Let their velocity functions be denoted by <m>c(t)</m> and <m>f(t)</m> respectively,
      where velocity is measured in centimeters per second and time <m>t</m>
      is measured in seconds.
    </p>

    <p>
      Open this <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/e8eobb7zep">Desmos interactive graph</url> and 
      use it to answer the following questions.
    </p>

    <ol marker="(a)">
      <li>
        During the race, what is the maximum velocity of each mouse?
      </li>
      <li>
        Which mouse was ahead <m>8</m> seconds into the race, and by how much?
      </li>
      <li>
        At what time during the race does Charlie first pull ahead of Frank?
      </li>
      <li>Who won the race (who is ahead at <m>t=30</m>)?</li>
    </ol>
  </statement>

  <solution>
    <p>(a) To find the maximum velocity of each mouse, look for the highest point on each velocity graph. 
      The y-coordinate of this point will give the maximum velocity in centimeters per second.
    </p>
    <p> For Charlie, the maximum velocity is approximately <m>116</m> cm/s, and for Frank, 
      the maximum velocity is approximately <m>150</m> cm/s. 
    </p>
    <p>  (b) To determine which mouse is ahead at time <m>t=8</m>, we need to compute the accumulated distance 
      traveled by each mouse up to that time. 
      The distance traveled by a mouse from time 0 to time 8 can be found by evaluating the definite integral of its velocity function:
    </p>
    <me> 
      \begin{aligned}
      \text{Distance traveled by Charlie} \amp = \int_0^8 c(t)\,dt \approx 775.5 \text{ cm}  \\
      \text{Distance traveled by Frank} \amp = \int_0^8 f(t)\,dt \approx 968.5 \text{ cm}
      \end{aligned}
    </me>

    <p>
      (c) To find when Charlie first pulls ahead of Frank, look for the first time <m>b</m> at which
      <m>\displaystyle \int_0^b c(t)\,dt</m> becomes larger than
      <m>\displaystyle \int_0^b f(t)\,dt</m>. Using the slider we can find that this occurs at approximately <m>t=15.9</m> seconds.
    </p>

    <p> (d) To determine who won the race, we need to compare the total distance traveled by each mouse at <m>t=30</m>. 
      This can be found by evaluating the definite integrals:
    </p>
    <me>
      \begin{aligned}
      \text{Total distance traveled by Charlie} \amp = \int_0^{30} c(t)\,dt \approx 3096.4 \text{ cm}  \\
      \text{Total distance traveled by Frank} \amp = \int_0^{30} f(t)\,dt \approx 2003.3 \text{ cm }
      \end{aligned}
    </me>
    <p>   Therefore, Charlie won the race
    </p> 


    
  </solution>
</example>




<example xml:id="ex-netchange-fish-population">
  <statement>
    <p>
      A fish population in a lake is <m>2500</m> at time <m>t=0</m>.
      The population is changing at a rate of
    </p>

    <me>
      r(t)=300e^{-t/5}
    </me>

    <p>
      fish per month, where <m>t</m> is measured in months.
      What is the fish population after <m>6</m> months?
    </p>
  </statement>

  <solution>
    <p>
      Since <m>r(t)</m> is the rate of change of the population, the total change in population
      from <m>t=0</m> to <m>t=6</m> is(according to the Net Change Theorem):
    </p>

    <me>
      P(6)-P(0)=
      \displaystyle \int_0^6 r(t)\,dt
      =
      \displaystyle \int_0^6 300e^{-t/5}\,dt.
    </me>

    <p>
      Now evaluate the integral (by finding the antiderivative).
    </p>

    <me>
      \displaystyle \int 300e^{-t/5}\,dt
      =
      -1500e^{-t/5}+C.
    </me>

    <p>
      Therefore the change in population from <m>t=0</m> to <m>t=6</m> is,
    </p>

    <me>
      \begin{aligned}
      P(6)-P(0) 
      \amp =\displaystyle \int_0^6 300e^{-t/5}\,dt\\
      \amp = \left.-1500e^{-t/5}\right|_0^6\\
      \amp =      -1500e^{-6/5}+1500
      \end{aligned}
    </me>

    <p>
      Thus, the fish population after <m>6</m> months is the initial population plus the change in population:
    </p>

    <me>
      \begin{aligned}
      P(6) \amp =P(0)+\left(-1500e^{-6/5}+1500\right)\\
      \amp =2500+\left(-1500e^{-6/5}+1500\right)\\
      \amp =4000-1500e^{-6/5}
      \end{aligned}
    </me>

    <p>
      Using a calculator to approximate,
    </p>

    <me>
      P(6)\approx 4000-1500(0.3010)\approx 3548.2.
    </me>

    <p>
      So after <m>6</m> months, the fish population is about <m>3548</m>. 
      If you did not have a calculator (like on an exam), you should leave the answer in the form <m>4000-1500e^{-6/5}</m>.
    </p>
  </solution>
</example>





    </subsection>

       <subsection xml:id="subsec-integral-usub">
      <title><xref ref="sec-integral-usub"/> Substitution Rule for Integration</title>

      
    <example xml:id="ex-sub-02">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Evaluate <m>\displaystyle \int x\sqrt{3x+4}\,dx</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Let <m>u=3x+4</m>, so <m>du=3\,dx</m> and <m>dx=\tfrac13\,du</m>.
          We also solve for <m>x</m>: <m>u=3x+4</m> gives <m>x=\tfrac13u-\tfrac43</m>.
        </p>

        <me>
          \begin{array}{rcl}
          \displaystyle \int x\sqrt{3x+4}\,dx
          &amp;=&amp;
          \displaystyle \int \left(\tfrac13u-\tfrac43\right)\sqrt{u}\cdot \tfrac13\,du
          \\[0.9em]
          &amp;=&amp;
          \displaystyle \int \left(\tfrac19u^{3/2}-\tfrac49u^{1/2}\right)\,du
          \\[0.9em]
          &amp;=&amp;
          \displaystyle \tfrac19\cdot \tfrac{2}{5}u^{5/2}-\tfrac49\cdot \tfrac{2}{3}u^{3/2}+C
          \\[0.9em]
          &amp;=&amp;
          \displaystyle \tfrac{2}{45}(3x+4)^{5/2}-\tfrac{8}{27}(3x+4)^{3/2}+C.
          \end{array}
        </me>
      </solution>
    </example>

   

    <example xml:id="ex-sub-04">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Evaluate <m>\displaystyle \int \frac{\sin(x)}{(1+\cos(x))^2}\,dx</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Let <m>u=1+\cos(x)</m>. Then <m>du=-\sin(x)\,dx</m>, so <m>\sin(x)\,dx=-du</m>.
        </p>

        <me>
          \begin{array}{rcl}
          \displaystyle \int \frac{\sin(x)}{(1+\cos(x))^2}\,dx
          &amp;=&amp;
          \displaystyle -\int \frac{1}{u^2}\,du
          \\[0.9em]
          &amp;=&amp;
          \displaystyle -\left(\frac{u^{-1}}{-1}\right)+C
          \ =\
          \frac{1}{u}+C
          \\[0.9em]
          &amp;=&amp;
          \displaystyle \frac{1}{1+\cos(x)}+C.
          \end{array}
        </me>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="ex-sub-05">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Evaluate <m>\displaystyle \int \sec^2(x)\tan(x)\,dx</m> in two ways, and explain why the answers agree.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Method 1: Let <m>u=\tan(x)</m>. Then <m>du=\sec^2(x)\,dx</m>.
        </p>

        <me>
          \begin{array}{rcl}
          \displaystyle \int \sec^2(x)\tan(x)\,dx
          &amp;=&amp;
          \displaystyle \int u\,du
          \ =\
          \tfrac12 u^2 + C
          \ =\
          \tfrac12\tan^2(x)+C.
          \end{array}
        </me>

        <p>
          Method 2: Write the integrand as <m>\sec(x)\cdot (\tan(x)\sec(x))</m> and let <m>z=\sec(x)</m>.
          Then <m>dz=\tan(x)\sec(x)\,dx</m>.
        </p>

        <me>
          \begin{array}{rcl}
          \displaystyle \int \sec^2(x)\tan(x)\,dx
          &amp;=&amp;
          \displaystyle \int z\,dz
          \ =\
          \tfrac12 z^2 + C
          \ =\
          \tfrac12\sec^2(x)+C.
          \end{array}
        </me>

        <p>
          These differ by a constant because <m>\sec^2(x)=\tan^2(x)+1</m>.
          So <m>\tfrac12\sec^2(x)=\tfrac12\tan^2(x)+\tfrac12</m>, meaning the two antiderivative families are the same.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="ex-sub-06">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Consider the function <m>f(x)=\dfrac{1}{x([\ln(x)]^2+1)}</m>
        </p>
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li>
            Find an antiderivative of <m>f(x)</m>.
          </li>
          <li>
            Evaluate <m>\displaystyle \int_1^e f(x)\,dx</m>.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          (a) Let <m>u=\ln(x)</m>. Then <m>du=\frac{1}{x}\,dx</m>.
        </p>
        <me>
          \begin{array}{rcl}
          \displaystyle \int f(x)\,dx
          &amp;=&amp;
          \displaystyle \int \frac{1}{x([\ln(x)]^2+1)}\,dx
          \\
          &amp;=&amp;
          \displaystyle \int \frac{1}{u^2+1}\,du 
          \\
          &amp;=&amp;
          \tan^{-1}(u)+C
          \\
          &amp;=&amp;
          \tan^{-1}(\ln(x))+C
          \end{array}
        </me>
        <p>
          (b) Now we can evaluate the definite integral:
        </p>
        <me>
          \begin{array}{rcl}
          \displaystyle \int_1^e f(x)\,dx
          &amp;=&amp;
          \displaystyle \left[\tan^{-1}(\ln(x))\right]_1^e
          \\
          &amp;=&amp;
          \tan^{-1}(\ln(e)) - \tan^{-1}(\ln(1))
          \\
          &amp;=&amp;
          \tan^{-1}(1) - \tan^{-1}(0)
          \\
          &amp;=&amp;
          \frac{\pi}{4} - 0
          \\
          &amp;=&amp;
          \frac{\pi}{4}
          \end{array}
        </me>
      </solution>
    </example>




    </subsection>


</section>